<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Promotion of Bantu Self-Government Act, 1959</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Promotion of Bantu Self-Government Act of 1959 was a South African law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a>'s government that divided the African population into ethnic 'national units', each to be given self-government in a homeland, or bantustan. It abolished the representation of Africans in parliament by white members and laid the basis for the nominal independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transkei">Transkei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bophuthatswana">Bophuthatswana</a>, Venda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciskei">Ciskei</a> between 1976 and 1981, which no other state recognised.</p></div>
